    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Mouvement des Entreprises Comoriennes (MODEC) conducted advocacy activities for influencing policy based on its analysis of the needs of the business environment. This analysis led MODEC to identify four key issues for enterprise growth: enhancing access to finance for SMEs, improving trading conditions, growing the Blue Economy and advancing female entrepreneurship. Initially, MODEC conducted a national survey, presenting its  findings at an advocacy training event held in Aug 2022. The participants leveraged the findings to lay the foundation for MODEC policy positions (30 participants). In parallel, a report on an Enabling Environment for Sustainable Enterprises (EESE) was developed. Allied to this was a &quot;Rapid situational analysis of the links between climate, environment and decent employment for a just transition towards a blue economy in the Comoros.&quot;  Utilizing data garnered from surveys, MODEC effectively promoted these issues with policymakers. A key recommendation of the MODEC survey was the need for firms to have resilience and contingency plans which could assist business sustainability. Subsequently, MODEC rolled out business resilience training for SME members (May-July 2023). Training manuals and other training materials were developed utilizing all collective information (EESE report, business resilience training, the national survey and the policy advocacy workshop recommendations). This material underwent validation at a workshop organized in 28 Aug 2023 attracting over 30 participants. MODEC developed Policy Positions for the Blue Economy Framework, in the Fisheries, Coastal Tourism and Waste Management sectors. Policy recommendations were validated by the MODEC membership at the General Assembly (June 2023). This policy framework proved influential across numerous fora with the government and was used consistently in discussions with government and unions in the national tripartite body.  In the run-up to the Blue economy forum for African Small The ILO supported a MODEC Board level retreat (May 2022) attended by all MODEC Board members to agree on policy priorities concerning enterprise growth. Subsequently, the ILO provided support to MODEC to run a national survey involving 185 respondents and organize six focus groups during June/July 2022. The ILO delivered a technical advocacy training for MODEC to develop concrete policy recommendations (Sept 2022). Additionally, the ILO supported the development of Comoros&#x2019; EESE report and further contributed to its validation through a workshop attended by 60 MODEC members in Sep 2022. Additionally, the ILO supported the development of the Rapid situational analysis (Blue Economy) as well as a tripartite validation workshop in October 2022 (48 participants). A separate capacity-building workshop for MODEC on this theme was held in October 2022 (55 participants). 
In collaboration with ITC-ILO, the ILO supported a Training of Trainers (ToT) for the business resilience training on Sustainable and Resilient Enterprises (SURE) in Nov-Dec 2022 resulting in 15 certified SURE trainers. Follow-up SURE-certified training for144 SMEs was conducted in Feb-March 2023 across Grande-Comore and Anjouan et Moh&#xe9;li. 
The ILO supported a total of six technical and consultative workshops (Grande-Comore, Anjouan et Moh&#xe9;li) to explore opportunities and challenges to the growth of the Blue Economy and measures to promote female entrepreneurship (Sept/Nov 2022-total 302 participants). The ILO supported training manuals and other training material on accessing finance and customs procedures along with the validation process.  The ILO supported MODEC in the development of specific policy positions on the Blue Economy. ITC-ILO also provided training support (EBMO Master course) to MODEC staff to build their advocacy and lobbying capacity (Nov 2023).</narrative>
   </description>
